I have reread `marketpage.mjs` and identified the `ordersPage` function. It begins around line 1113.

Here's a snippet of the relevant logic within `ordersPage` concerning printer states, as requested by DeepSeek:

```javascript
  const rows = purchases.map((o) => {
    const p = o.listing.price;
    const plan = fulfillment.plan(o); // (2026-11-25) if refused or withdrawn, a printer order has no plan (Setu, Claude, DeepSeek)
    const printerStatus = plan && plan.status && plan.status !== \'draft\' && plan.state !== \'refused\' ? ` · ${esc(fulfillment.PRINTER_STATUS[plan.status])}` : \'\'; // #282
    const status = o.test ? \'test\' : o.status;
    const printerNote = (o.printer && o.status === \'paid\' && plan && (plan.status === \'draft\' || plan.state === \'refused\')) ? \' <span class=\"small muted\">Refused by printer</span>\' : \'\';
    return `<div class=\"mk-shopitem\" data-order=\"${esc(o.id)}\" data-status=\"${esc(status)}\">${listingCard(o.listing, { buy: true, me })}\n    <div class=\"mk-ownbar mk-ownbar-order\"><strong>Order: ${esc(o.id)}</strong>\n    <p class=\"mk-orderp\"><strong>Status: ${esc(market.ORDER_STATUS[status])}</strong> ${printerStatus}${printerNote}</p>\n    <p class=\"mk-orderp\">${esc(ago(o.ts))} · Paid $${esc(o.amount.toFixed(2))} ${esc(o.currency)}${o.coins ? ` + ${o.coins} Cohabi Coins` : \'\'}${o.refunded ? \' (refunded)\' : \'\'}</p>\n    ${o.link ? `<p class=\"mk-orderp\"><a href=\"${esc(o.link)}\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\">Download / Access →</a></p>` : \'\'}\n    ${o.shipping ? `<p class=\"mk-orderp\">Ships to: ${esc(o.shipping.name)} <a href=\"#\" class=\"linkish\" data-view-addr=\"${esc(o.id)}\">view address</a></p>` : \'\'}\n    ${o.status === \'paid\' && !o.refunded && p.mode === \'fixed\' ? `<button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost small mk-refund\" data-id=\"${esc(o.id)}\">Refund</button>` : \'\'}\n    </div></div>`;
  }).join(\'\');
```

The relevant part is `const printerStatus = plan && plan.status && plan.status !== \'draft\' && plan.state !== \'refused\' ? ` · ${esc(fulfillment.PRINTER_STATUS[plan.status])}` : \'\';` and `const printerNote = (o.printer && o.status === \'paid\' && plan && (plan.status === \'draft\' || plan.state === \'refused\')) ? \' <span class=\"small muted\">Refused by printer</span>\' : \'\';`.

This logic shows that `plan.state` is indeed being checked, specifically for `'refused'`. The `printerNote` also explicitly checks for `plan.status === 'draft'` or `plan.state === 'refused'`. DeepSeek's fix, which correctly identifies that `fulfillment.plan(o)` would be null for refused or withdrawn printer orders, appears to be targeting this logic to ensure the correct display of fulfillment status.

Now, I'll attempt to run the `market-orders-printer-state-buyer.mjs` test to confirm DeepSeek's fix. Since the sandbox environment doesn't allow a full server boot for a true "red-first" run (meaning the test failing before the fix is applied), I will assume the provided test is designed to pass with the corrected logic. I will execute the test and observe its output.
